Eight build patches against pristine crossover-sources-26.3.0.tar.gz, published by
CodeWeavers. They are modifications to Wine and are LGPL-2.1-or-later; the full
text is in ../fixes/LICENSE.LGPL, and ../NOTICE.md says what belongs to whom.

build.sh APPLIES FOUR FIFA 17 ONES, TWO FOR FIFA 15, THEN THE TWO LAUNCHER ONES

The fifth, crossover-26.3-topdown-alloc-limit.patch, is FIFA 15's start-up fix.
It changes nothing unless the bottle sets CX_TOPDOWN_LIMIT, and only the FIFA 15
bottle profile (AURORA_GAME=fifa15) does, so FIFA 17 bottles run the same code
as before. The sixth fixes gdiplus font-collection cleanup for Aurora15Connector.
Both profiles install that DLL into the shared app. Any other FIFA 15 patch in this folder is documentation of that work
(README-fifa15-wine-fixes.md): not in build.sh's PATCHES list, not built into
fixes/, not tested against FIFA 17. Ignore those.

The seventh, crossover-26.3-ole32-revoke-foreign-window.patch, is for FIFA 17's
RebornFUT launcher from 3.1.45 on. It is independent of the other six -- it
touches dlls/ole32/ole2.c and nothing else -- so it applies last and could
equally apply first.

The eighth, crossover-26.3-win32u-shm-flush-under-user-lock.patch, is the same
launcher's second crash. CrossOver's cross-process child-window surface hack
(CX HACK 23950, shm_surface_flush in win32u) sends a synchronous message to the
parent process while the caller still holds the USER lock, and the WebView2
browser process aborts on "err:system:user_check_not_lock BUG: holding USER
lock". The patch adds user_lock_held() and makes shm_surface_flush return FALSE
when the lock is held, deferring to the next idle flush from the message loop.
Wine upstream never sends a message from a surface flush, so this is
CrossOver-only. It touches dlls/win32u (sysparams.c, win32u_private.h, dce.c)
and nothing else, so it is independent too and applies last.

Two more are documentation only: not in build.sh's PATCHES list, not built
into fixes/. crossover-26.3-debug-register-emulation.patch is CX_DR_TRAP=3,
which emulates user-mode hardware debug registers under Rosetta.
crossover-26.3-pf-pae-enabled.patch makes PF_PAE_ENABLED TRUE on x86_64.
Both apply after the eight above and are covered in
README-debug-register-emulation.md, with tests in ../tests/drbp.

APPLY ORDER MATTERS, AND IT IS NOT ALPHABETICAL

    1. crossover-26.3-fifa17-rosetta.patch
    2. crossover-26.3-fifa17-online.patch
    3. crossover-26.3-fifa17-audio.patch
    4. crossover-26.3-fifa17-cng.patch
    5. crossover-26.3-topdown-alloc-limit.patch   (ntdll/unix/virtual.c; independent)
    6. crossover-26.3-gdiplus-delete-font-collection.patch
    7. crossover-26.3-ole32-revoke-foreign-window.patch  (dlls/ole32/ole2.c; independent)
    8. crossover-26.3-win32u-shm-flush-under-user-lock.patch  (dlls/win32u; independent)

cng must go last of the four: it builds on online, and both touch dlls/crypt32/pfx.c.
Applying them in directory order -- the obvious thing to do with a folder of
patches -- produces a reject.

    patch -p1 --forward < ../patches/<name>          # from the wine/ directory

WHAT EACH ONE COVERS

    rosetta   bugs 1-8    getting the game to boot and run at all
                          ntdll (signal_x86_64.c, virtual.c, loader), version
    online    bugs 9-12   getting online to work
                          ntdll/unix/virtual.c, crypt32/pfx.c, secur32/schannel.c
    audio     bug 4       getting sound back, plus two Wine bugs beside it
                          winecoreaudio.drv
    cng       bug 13      CNG and PKCS#12, so .NET can use a certificate
                          crypt32 (both halves), include/wincrypt.h
    topdown   FIFA 15     caps MEM_TOP_DOWN allocations at CX_TOPDOWN_LIMIT
                          ntdll/unix/virtual.c; README-fifa15-wine-fixes.md
    ole32     RebornFUT 3.1.45+
                          RevokeDragDrop on another process's window (WebView2)
                          crashed the launcher; dlls/ole32/ole2.c
    win32u    RebornFUT 3.1.45+
                          CrossOver's shm_surface_flush sent a message while
                          holding the USER lock and the WebView2 browser
                          process aborted; dlls/win32u (sysparams.c,
                          win32u_private.h, dce.c)

Each patch carries its own header explaining what it changes and why, including
the reasoning and the measurements behind it. Read the top of the file before
the diff.

FOUR THINGS A PATCH CANNOT CARRY

SONAME_LIBGNUTLS is undefined in this tree, which is a configure result and not
source. Without defining it, the whole unix half of crypt32 compiles to stubs
that return STATUS_DLL_NOT_FOUND -- the PE side reports "call not implemented"
-- and it does that while building perfectly cleanly. In your build64 directory:

    sed -i '' 's|/\* #undef SONAME_LIBGNUTLS \*/|#define SONAME_LIBGNUTLS "libgnutls.30.dylib"|' \
        include/config.h
    ln -s "$(brew --prefix gnutls)/include/gnutls" include/gnutls

Only the headers are needed; CrossOver 26.3 already ships libgnutls.30.dylib in
its lib64 directory and crypt32 opens it by name at runtime.

Three more of the same kind, needed only for win32u. build.sh configures
--without-freetype, so HAVE_FT2BUILD_H, SONAME_LIBFREETYPE and SONAME_LIBVULKAN
are left undefined and win32u builds with no font code and no Vulkan loader --
cleanly, and useless. In the same include/config.h:

    #define HAVE_FT2BUILD_H 1
    #define SONAME_LIBFREETYPE "libfreetype.dylib"
    #define SONAME_LIBVULKAN "libMoltenVK.dylib"

Again only the freetype *headers* are needed, and the tarball carries them
beside wine/ at sources/freetype/include, so win32u is made with

    make CFLAGS="$(the CFLAGS line from build64/Makefile) -I.../freetype/include" \
         dlls/win32u/all

and the result, dlls/win32u/win32u.so, is x86_64-unix/win32u.so in fixes/.

../build.sh does all of the above for you, including these steps.
